  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSatellite imagery shows one final patch of stratus eroding\nacross the southwestern portions of the forecast area this\nafternoon, with cumulus fields and sunshine prevailing\nelsewhere. High pressure takes center stage for the first half\nof the work week, beginning over the Great Lakes today and\ngradually sliding towards the eastern seaboard for Tuesday and\nWednesday. Coupled with upper level ridging aloft, mostly dry\nweather is anticipated to rule the roost through midweek.\n\nIn the process of the surface high's eastward journey, a warming\ntrend is set to ensue. A taste of fall is currently underway\ntoday across the Central Appalachians, with daytime highs in the\n70s and dew points in the 50s/60s. Temperatures more indicative\nof summertime then triumph over the region for the rest of the \nwork week. Heat and humidity will impose afternoon heat indices\nstretching back into the upper 90s for many locations across the\nlowlands by Wednesday and Thursday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe second half of the work week into the weekend turns more\nactive as the upper level ridge breaks down and allows a front\nboundary to drape down from the north. While uncertainty remains\non whether the front makes a complete passage through the area\nthen returning as a warm front or wobbles overhead through the\nupcoming weekend, model ensembles signal daily chances of\nshowers and storms within the presence of this boundary.\nEmbedded shortwaves flowing along the front will serve up\npotential for severe weather and flash flooding concerns across\nthe Ohio Valley at times, consistent with previous active\npatterns the region has faced within the past month.",
